---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description="About Utkarsh Thaokar, DevOps & Cloud Engineer.">
  <div class="prose">
  <h1>About</h1>

  <p>
    I'm Utkarsh &mdash; a DevOps &amp; Cloud Engineer based in Pune, India, with about
    4.5 years across AWS and Azure. I like the parts of engineering most people
    try to avoid: the pipeline that's flaking for no obvious reason, the disk
    that mysteriously filled up overnight, the Terraform plan that shows a diff
    nobody expected. Figuring out <em>why</em> is usually more interesting than
    the fix itself.
  </p>

  <p>
    Right now I'm the sole DevOps owner at <strong>Netnology Solutions</strong>,
    where I built the CI/CD, containerization, and deployment automation for a
    platform that had none of it in place before &mdash; GitLab CI/CD across
    multiple environments, Docker/Compose for dev-staging-prod parity, and
    centralized logging with the ELK stack. Before that I was a Site Reliability
    Engineer at <strong>Triguna Legal</strong>, standing up monitoring and access
    controls for a compliance-sensitive legal environment, and a Cloud Consultant
    at <strong>Rapid Circle</strong>, where I worked on a global Oil &amp; Gas
    client's Azure estate (Shell, Amsterdam) &mdash; migrating provisioning to
    Terraform and chasing down state drift that kept causing failures no one
    could explain.
  </p>

  <p>
    I've also been building outside of work hours: <a href="/freelance/">The Film
    Fraternity</a>, a full-stack platform for India's film crews to publish
    portfolios, get discovered, and rent equipment from each other &mdash; my
    first time owning a product end-to-end, frontend to backend to deploy.
  </p>

  <p>
    This site is where I write about the infrastructure problems I run into,
    keep an honest record of what I've actually built (see <a href="/resume/">Resume</a>),
    and post freelance and collaboration work as it happens. If you want the
    long version with numbers and dates, that's the resume page. If you want the
    "here's what actually went wrong and how I fixed it" version, that's the
    <a href="/blog/">blog</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    Outside of infrastructure: I'm from Pune, and I'm generally more comfortable
    debugging a broken deploy at midnight than writing a bio about myself in the
    third person, so if this page feels a little dry, that's why.
  </p>

  <p>
    <a href="/contact/">Get in touch &rarr;</a>
  </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
